A Personal Trust Control Plane

Author: Yusuke Hayashi
OpenPGP: B22B 98AB B2D5 0330 7AB6 A316 0718 EFA6 506B B669
Canonical: https://yusuke-hayashi.com/trust-control-plane
Published: 2026-07-21

How I made my public identity verifiable, monitorable, and recoverable,
without depending on any single platform.


THE PROBLEM

Most of what proves "who I am" as an engineer lives inside platforms:
a verified badge here, an OAuth login there, a blue checkmark somewhere
else. Each of these works until the platform changes its policy,
suspends the account, gets acquired, or shuts down. None of them
compose: my GitHub account says nothing cryptographically about my
domain, my domain says nothing about my Ethereum address, and if I lost
a key tomorrow, none of them would tell you what to trust instead.

I wanted something that answers four questions, verifiably and durably:

1. Who currently controls each public identifier?
2. What is the current canonical state, and how can a third party
   verify it?
3. What changed over time, and how can earlier states be audited?
4. If a key, device, or account is compromised, or I am unavailable,
   how do revocation and migration happen?

This article describes the system I built to answer them: a personal
trust control plane. Not a product, not a protocol proposal — a working
setup for one person, with the design decisions and the mistakes made
along the way.


CONTROL PLANE VS. DISTRIBUTION PLANE

The first structural decision: separate where identity is managed from
where it is served.

* A private control-plane repository holds the expected identity
  definition, signing targets, pre-publication artifacts, verification
  code, runbooks, and an audit trail of every change. It contains no
  secrets — no seeds, no private keys, no passphrases. It manages
  policy and procedure, not key material.
* A public distribution plane (this website and its public repository)
  serves what third parties actually fetch: keys, signed manifests,
  proofs, and a verifier.

This separation means the public site can be rebuilt from scratch after
a compromise, because the source of truth for "what should be
published" never lived there.


KEYS: AN OFFLINE ROOT AND MACHINE-SCOPED SUBKEYS

The OpenPGP layout is deliberately boring:

* An Ed25519 certify-only primary key that stays offline and is used
  only to manage subkeys and identities.
* Machine-scoped signing subkeys: each machine holds its own signing
  subkey's private material, and no private subkey is shared across
  machines. A compromised laptop means revoking that laptop's subkeys,
  not rebuilding my identity.
* Separate encryption and SSH-authentication subkeys.

My previous RSA-4096 key (2020-2026) was revoked as superseded, not
silently abandoned — the revocation is published through the same
channels as the key itself, so verifiers see an explicit transition
rather than a dangling old key.


MACHINE-READABLE IDENTITY, SIGNED AND HISTORY-LINKED

Human-readable "about" pages rot. The canonical state is instead a
machine-readable manifest at a well-known URL:

* /.well-known/identity.json — every current identifier (OpenPGP
  fingerprint, Ethereum address, ENS name, DNS domains, Nostr, Bluesky,
  GitHub), plus expiry dates, verifier hash, and history references.
* A detached OpenPGP signature over the exact manifest bytes.
* An append-only history: every change to the manifest is an event, and
  old states are published as content-addressed checkpoints, so
  rewriting the past is detectable.
* A pinned public verifier: a dependency-free script whose SHA-256 is
  embedded in the signed manifest. Anyone can run it and check every
  claim — no API keys, no secrets, no trust in my CI.


THE TRUST BOOTSTRAP PROBLEM

There is a circularity trap in self-hosted identity: if you fetch my
key, my manifest, and my verifier all from the same website, then an
attacker who fully controls the site can hand you a consistent set of
lies.

The bootstrap procedure therefore never treats the site alone as a
trust root. A first-time verifier is told to compare the fingerprint
across channels with different failure domains — DNS TXT records on two
separate domains, WKD on a third domain, the OpenPGP signature on the
public repository's main branch — and ideally against an out-of-band
copy (a business card, a previously known signature). The verification
instructions explicitly record which channel you started from, because
the guarantee you get depends on it. Honest limits are part of the
design: the procedure documents what it does NOT prove (legal identity,
global uniqueness, device security), not just what it does.


BRIDGING OPENPGP AND ETHEREUM

Neither PGP nor Ethereum depends on a platform, so they make a good
pair — but they know nothing about each other. The bridge is a pair of
signed statements: the OpenPGP key signs a statement naming the
Ethereum address, and the Ethereum key signs a statement naming the
OpenPGP fingerprint. Both are published and re-verified continuously,
including signature recovery back to the exact address. Either
signature alone would be spoofable by someone who controls just one
side; the pair binds them.


ENS IS AN ADAPTER, NOT A FOUNDATION

This was the decision I got wrong first. The initial plan treated ENS
registration as a prerequisite. It is not: ENS is a name-resolution
adapter on top of an Ethereum address that must already be verifiable
without it. I re-planned around the principle that an ENS or IPFS
outage must never block signing, recovery, or attestation, then
registered yhay81.eth later, as an add-on.

The ENS profile itself is two-layered: the contenthash points to an
immutable IPFS capsule (a snapshot that can never silently change),
while the capsule points back to the canonical HTTPS site for current
revocation state and complete proofs. Immutability where it helps,
freshness where it matters.


MONITORING: CHECKS THAT STATE THEIR EXACT GUARANTEE

A weekly healthcheck verifies the whole surface using public
information only: manifest signature, history integrity, verifier hash,
cross-attestations, WKD, DNS proofs, Nostr, Bluesky, GitHub commit
signatures, ENS records, and OpenTimestamps artifacts. Two design rules
turned out to matter more than the check count:

1. Every check states its exact guarantee. The OpenTimestamps check,
   for example, reports "timestamp artifacts present" separately from
   full cryptographic verification, instead of a vague green checkmark.
   A check that overstates its guarantee is worse than no check.
2. Expiry becomes "degraded" 30 days early. Keys, manifests,
   security.txt, and ENS registration all expire; the system nags me
   before third parties see anything broken.

The healthcheck runs in CI — but CI is itself a platform dependency, so
an independent watchdog runs on my own machine outside GitHub Actions,
and verifies the hash of its own installed verifier against the signed
manifest (a stale or tampered monitor is itself a failure mode).


RECOVERY IS A FEATURE, NOT AN AFTERTHOUGHT

The system assumes keys leak, devices vanish, and people become
unavailable. Prepared responses include:

* Scenario-based runbooks: device compromise, subkey vs. master-key
  leak, Ethereum seed leak (an address cannot be revoked — only
  migrated from), account takeovers, and death/disaster handover.
* An offline recovery kit: signed git bundles of both repositories,
  runbooks, and public evidence, with a checksum manifest and a
  detached OpenPGP signature. The generator refuses to run on a dirty
  worktree, so a kit always corresponds to a signed, published state.
* Envelope separation for physical handover: secrets in one sealed
  envelope, procedures and contacts in another, so a successor can
  locate and understand before opening anything sensitive.


A MATURITY MODEL INSTEAD OF CHECKBOXES

The single most useful process decision: no capability is "done".
Each one is tracked through five states —

Designed -> Implemented -> Published -> Monitored -> Rehearsed

— and the honest answer is that some of mine currently sit at
"implemented but not yet rehearsed", and the status table says so. The
same honesty applies to attestations: the framework for third-party
attestations exists and is continuously verified, and the health report
states plainly that zero real attestations exist yet. A trust system
that rounds "framework exists" up to "verified" would be lying about
exactly the thing it is supposed to guarantee.


DELEGATING OPERATIONS TO AN AI, EXPLICITLY

Much of this infrastructure is operated together with an AI coding
agent. That could easily undermine the whole point ("who controls the
key?"), so the delegation boundary is explicit and published in the key
policy:

* After I review and approve exact content, the agent may run signing
  commands using machine-scoped subkeys, regenerate manifests, and run
  verification.
* Only I perform master-key ceremonies, authorize production
  publishing, send on-chain transactions, and handle secrets and
  physical media.
* The agent never sees private key material or passphrases — signing
  goes through the local key agent.
* Every commit is OpenPGP-signed, so the git history is also an audit
  trail of what the agent executed.

In 2026, "who controls this identity" increasingly includes "what is
delegated to machines". Writing the boundary down — and publishing it —
seems more honest than pretending the delegation does not exist.


WHAT I WOULD TELL SOMEONE STARTING THIS

1. Start from the four questions, not from tools. ENS, Keyoxide, and
   badges are adapters; control, verifiability, history, and recovery
   are the actual requirements.
2. Track "implemented", "published", "monitored", and "rehearsed" as
   different states. The gap between them is where trust systems
   quietly fail.
3. Make every check state its exact guarantee — including what it does
   not prove.
4. Plan the exit first. Revocation, migration, and handover are the
   parts you cannot retrofit after the incident.
5. Keep the non-goals visible. No badge collecting, no single trust
   score, no premature generalization into a framework nobody asked
   for.


